# Feedback loop

A **feedback loop** is an arrangement in which the output of a system
influences its own later input. The system's state at one moment is partly a
consequence of what it produced earlier. Feedback is the ordinary mechanism of
control, learning and iteration, and it is the concept most often confused
with a [[strange-loop]].

## Description

A feedback loop has a signal path that closes on itself: act, observe the
result, adjust, act again. Two broad kinds are distinguished.

- **Negative feedback** opposes deviation and so stabilises. A thermostat
  turns the heating off as the room approaches the set temperature.
- **Positive feedback** reinforces deviation and so amplifies. A microphone
  placed near its own loudspeaker howls.

Software development is full of feedback loops. A compiler or proof checker
rejects a program, the author revises it, and the checker runs again. In work
with language models the same loop runs from intention to specification to
program to execution to observation and back to a revised specification. The
stricter the checker, the more information each turn of the loop returns.

What a feedback loop lacks is any change of level. Every signal in the loop is
the same kind of thing as every other: a temperature, a test result, a new
revision of a text. Nothing in the loop has to represent the loop. For that
reason a cycle of document, code, output and document again is feedback and
nothing more, however many times it runs. It becomes a candidate strange loop
only when the document represents the mechanism that interprets it and the
interpretation changes that same mechanism.

The discussion reached this point by correcting itself. An early description
of executable documents treated any system that keeps modifying itself as a
strange loop. The objection was immediate: then every dynamic system is one.
The equivalence was withdrawn, and the same caution is repeated wherever the
vault describes a loop it wants to build. A system in which a document
describes a computation, the computation produces information, and the
information changes the document has interacting levels, and is still only
feedback until its representations of its own rules take part in changing
those rules.

The related notions line up by what each one adds.

| Concept | What distinguishes it |
|---|---|
| Dynamic system | State changes over time |
| Feedback loop | Output influences later input |
| Recursion | A computation is defined through a smaller instance of itself |
| Self-modifying program | The rules of behaviour can change |
| Strange loop | A hierarchy of representation is crossed and returned through |

## Relations

- It is not a [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: it has a cycle but no change of level.
- It is not [[recursion]] {relation: distinguished-from}: recursion is a definition through smaller instances, feedback a causal cycle in time.
- It becomes reflective only when combined with [[level-crossing]] {relation: contrasts}.
- The [[hmd-feedback-loop]] {relation: example-of} is the instance built from cards, cells and revisions.
- The [[reasoning-cycle]] {relation: example-of} is the instance built from hypotheses, predictions and tests.
- [[revision]] {relation: uses} is the step that closes the loop on a model.
- [[human-in-the-loop]] {relation: uses} places a person at the step where the result is interpreted.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}, as the baseline the experiment has to exceed.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[strange-loop]].** A loop that passes through a representation of the
  system that is looping, so that levels of description are crossed. Feedback
  is the part of a strange loop that is not strange.
- **[[hmd-feedback-loop]].** A card holds knowledge and instructions, a cell
  is run, the result is written beside it, a person or agent interprets the
  result, and an accepted revision becomes the next version of the card.
- **[[reasoning-cycle]].** Observations lead by induction or abduction to a
  hypothesis, deduction turns the hypothesis into predictions, and testing
  returns new observations.
- **[[recursion]].** A definition that refers to a smaller instance of what it
  defines. It terminates at a base case, where feedback may run indefinitely.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[feedback-loop]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[feedback-loop]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

Future lens that applies to this card: [[find-level-crossings]] separates the
link cycles in this vault that are plain feedback from those that cross levels.

